    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">The brief</h2>
      <p>
        Rainy Days needed a straightforward e-commerce experience for an outdoor
        jacket brand. Users had to move from browsing and comparing products to
        product details, cart, checkout, and confirmation with a clear sense of
        progress. The visual direction needed to feel reliable and
        outdoors-focused while keeping the products central.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <div class="case-study-grid">
        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--challenge">
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            An online shop repeats similar patterns across many screens, so
            small inconsistencies can quickly make the experience feel
            uncertain. The challenge was to maintain clear hierarchy and action
            language from the first product card through forms and checkout
            confirmation, especially on smaller screens.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Support fast product scanning without overcrowding cards</li>
            <li>Keep the next action obvious throughout a multi-step purchase
              journey</li>
            <li>Adapt navigation, grids, forms, and checkout content for mobile
              use</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--explore">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/web-rainydays-600.webp"
            data-src="web-rainydays-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="515"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Rainy Days mobile designs showing product discovery, details, cart, checkout, and confirmation screens"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Mobile screens across the complete
            shopping journey</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--decisions">
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Used consistent product cards, pricing treatment, buttons, and
              form fields to make repeated actions predictable throughout the
              shop
            </li>
            <li>
              Gave product imagery and essential purchasing details the
              strongest visual priority, supported by restrained colour and
              spacing
            </li>
            <li>
              Designed calls to action with clear hierarchy so users can
              distinguish primary purchase steps from secondary navigation
            </li>
            <li>
              Simplified layouts and increased spacing around controls on
              smaller screens to support comfortable touch interaction
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--booking">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/web-rainydays-desktop-600.webp"
            data-src="web-rainydays-desktop-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="626"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Rainy Days desktop product listing design with jacket cards, prices, and calls to action"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Desktop product grid with consistent
            cards and clear visual hierarchy</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class="case-study-block">
          <h2>Purchase flow</h2>
          <p>
            The high-fidelity prototype connected the screens as one journey
            rather than treating checkout as a separate design task. Cart
            visibility, form feedback, and confirmation give users context
            before, during, and after the purchase action.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Mapped browse → product details → cart → checkout → confirmation</li>
            <li>Included empty-cart and validation feedback in the planned
              experience</li>
            <li>Kept order information visible long enough for users to verify
              their choices</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/web-rainydays-flow-600.webp"
            data-src="web-rainydays-flow-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="346"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Rainy Days responsive e-commerce screens showing product details, checkout, and confirmation"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">High-fidelity flow from product
            evaluation to checkout confirmation</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row case-outcome">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The result is a cohesive responsive design for the complete purchase
        journey, supported by reusable interface patterns and predictable action
        hierarchy. As an earlier course project, Rainy Days is intentionally
        presented as a concise case: it demonstrates the foundation of my
        e-commerce thinking while newer projects show how that approach has
        developed further.
      </p>
    </div>

